    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **42 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the optional, validated `postcode` field on the owner-create endpoint (`POST /api/owners`), keeping the request contract backward-compatible.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an optional `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema with `pattern: '^[0-9]{4}$'`. Because `Owner` (the response DTO) inherits from `OwnerFields`, this makes postcode both accepted on input and returned on output. The `@Pattern` on the generated DTO makes a malformed value (e.g. `\"12\"`) a 400 \u2014 but only when the field is present (Bean Validation `@Pattern` passes on `null`), so it stays optional.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` field with `@Column(name = \"postcode\")` and getter/setter so MapStruct auto-maps it in both directions (store from `OwnerFieldsDto`, return in `OwnerDto`).\n\n3. **DB schemas** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added a `postcode` column to the `owners` table so the value persists.\n\n4. **`Postcodes.java`** (new common helper) \u2014 The pinned region\u2192range table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099), reusing `Localities.of(city)` to derive the region. Returns valid for a `null` postcode or a city with no known region (permissive default).\n\n5. **`ValidateOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 After the schema-format check, added a `validatePostcode` step that flags an out-of-range postcode as a `postcode` field error \u2192 400, consistent with the existing field-error escalation.\n\nThe region-range check runs only when the postcode is present and well-formed, matching the spec: validated when present, optional when absent, unknown-region cities accept any 4-digit postcode. All existing tests (including the minimal owners that send no postcode) still pass.",
